								<div class="title" id="post-title-2829041">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sinn1sl0ken" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2019868); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2019868&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606984881" alt="sinn1sl0ken" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2019868)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sinn1sl0ken" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2019868); return false;" >sinn1sl0ken</a></span> <span class="odate time_1495458431 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 May 2017 13:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2829041">
									

<p>I initially read this from a direct link to the ESCP page and I unconditionally enjoyed it. However, upon reading the base SCP and the note about ESCP in the general case, I want to qualify my vote.</p>
<p>I <em>particularly</em> enjoyed the concept of the procedures changing when the stakes are high because I thought of it as part of Envelope Logistics' influence on the Foundation; they were forcing the Foundation to publicize and legitimize EL's messages in times of danger, which is scary because it shows that the Foundation can be reliant on the messages while also giving EL a reason to (causatively?) predict disaster in order to maintain its influence. It fit well with the theme of 2557 being able to actively interfere with Foundation files while remaining at least superficially compliant with the format. If ESCP was something used outside the context of &quot;outside organization is changing how we write our pages&quot;, it would cheapen the impact of this article in my eyes because a lot of why this change feels significant is because it shows how much the Foundation is leaning on an outside power.</p>
<p>If I had started reading this from the base SCP, I don't know that I would have liked it as much. The pacing felt much better in the ESCP and you got the sense that something was wrong very quickly while still being able to read how the machine was used in states of nonemergency via the struck through text.</p>
<p>I loved the concept of market forces influencing an omniscient entity to inform the Foundation and the implication that disasters could provoke short sales was a great kicker to an already compelling article. However, this is the kind of screw that I feel should be specific to Envelope Logistic skips in general (or at the very least articles that are clearly being influenced by a third party). That aside, my initial +1 stands, this was very fun to read.</p>

							</div>

								
